      <!-- 2. How to Calculate Number of Bricks -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          How to Calculate the Number of Bricks for a Wall
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Using a <strong>number of bricks calculator</strong> or <strong
            >bricks calculator for wall</strong
          > construction relies on calculating the total masonry volume and dividing
          it by the nominal volume of an individual brick, which includes its surrounding
          mortar joint.
        </p>
        <div
          class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900"
        >
          <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            Standard Volumetric Step-by-Step Method:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
            <li>
              <strong>Gross Wall Area:</strong> Length × Height × Number of Walls
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Net Wall Area:</strong> Gross Wall Area − Total Opening Deductions
              (Doors, Windows, Voids)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Brickwork Volume:</strong> Net Wall Area × Wall Thickness
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nominal Brick Dimensions:</strong> (Brick Length + Mortar Joint)
              × (Brick Width + Mortar Joint) × (Brick Height + Mortar Joint)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Base Brick Count:</strong> Brickwork Volume / Nominal Single
              Brick Volume
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Final Brick Count:</strong> Base Brick Count × (1 + Wastage
              % / 100) (rounded up to nearest whole brick)
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. How to Calculate Mortar Quantity -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          How to Calculate Mortar Quantity for Brickwork
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Mortar fills the joints between bricks to transfer vertical and
          lateral loads. Estimating cement and sand involves finding the volume
          left unoccupied by the solid bricks:
        </p>
        <ol
          class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed"
        >
          <li>
            <strong>Calculate Total Masonry Volume:</strong> Determine the overall
            volume of the finished wall.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Determine Solid Brick Volume:</strong> Multiply the total brick
            count by the actual brick volume without mortar (Length × Width × Height).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Calculate Wet Mortar Volume:</strong> Subtract the solid brick
            volume from the total masonry volume. In standard modular brickwork, wet
            mortar accounts for approximately 23% to 30% of total wall volume.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Apply Dry Volume Multiplier:</strong> Dry cement and sand shrink
            when mixed with water and fill microscopic voids. A standard dry volume
            multiplier factor of <strong>1.33</strong> (a 33% increase) is applied
            along with a 10% wastage factor.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Apportion Cement and Sand:</strong> Divide the dry volume by the
            sum of parts in the mix ratio (e.g., 1 + 6 = 7 parts for a 1:6 mix). Multiply
            the cement volume by cement density (1,440 kg/m³) to determine total kilograms
            and 50 kg bags. Sand volume is converted into cubic feet (CFT) and metric
            tonnes (1,600 kg/m³ density).
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. Brickwork Calculator India -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          Brickwork Calculator for Indian Construction
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          When using a <strong>brickwork calculator india</strong> context, civil
          engineers and contractors encounter two primary brick categories recognized
          under Indian Standard codes (IS 1077 and IS 2212):
        </p>
        <ul
          class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed"
        >
          <li>
            <strong>Standard Modular Bricks:</strong> Manufactured with dimensions
            of 190 × 90 × 90 mm. With a standard 10 mm mortar joint, their nominal
            dimensions become 200 × 100 × 100 mm, yielding exactly <strong
              >500 bricks per cubic meter (m³)</strong
            > of masonry work.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Traditional Country Red Clay Bricks:</strong> Frequently fired
            in local kilns with nominal sizes of 225 × 112.5 × 75 mm (commonly called
            9-inch × 4.25-inch × 3-inch bricks). Because traditional bricks are slightly
            larger, roughly 430 to 460 bricks are consumed per cubic meter.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
          <em
            >Note: Brick sizes vary by state, kiln technology, and local
            manufacturer molds across India. Always measure a sample batch on
            site before finalizing procurement orders.</em
          >
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Paving Bricks Calculator -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
