      <!-- 7. Common Mistakes -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2
          class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
        >
          Common Mistakes When Estimating Brickwork
        </h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Forgetting Doors and Windows
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Failing to deduct large openings, archways, and structural column
              cutouts leads to substantial over-purchasing and extra material
              storage costs.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Ignoring Mortar Joint Thickness
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Calculating bricks using pure dry dimensions instead of nominal
              joint dimensions (+10 mm) overestimates the brick count by 15% to
              25%.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Using the Wrong Brick Dimensions
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Assuming all bricks are modular metric when using regional country
              red clay bricks or oversized engineering bricks results in
              significant calculation errors.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Not Including Wastage
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Transport breakage, half-bat cuts at wall ends, and handling
              damage require a 5% to 10% contingency buffer to prevent work
              stoppages.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Mixing Units
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Entering wall lengths in feet while specifying brick dimensions in
              millimeters without conversion leads to distorted volume outputs.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800"
          >
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              Assuming Material Consumption Is Exact
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Mortar consumption increases when brick frogs (indentations) are
              deep or when joints are raked out for sand-faced plastering.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
